      "body": "Quick Answer\n\nThe Tunnel Escape Fates Entwined story follows a military operative named Olivenia as she is escorted through a zombie virus-infected city. Her handler, Noah, gives her remote guidance from another location. The mission surfaces a researcher named Beatrice and hints at a hidden biological weapon. No further lore is published.\n\nTunnel Escape Fates Entwined story: the elevator pitch\n\nThe Steam store Description for AppID 4285110 summarises the game as a zombie-virus escort mission framed around an operative and her remote handler. The description is short and reads more like a hook than a chapter log, and it leans on the city backdrop rather than a chapter-by-chapter plot. The store page does not publish a full chapter list, an in-game timeline, a codex entry, or a synopsis of every act.\n\nFor the launch window, the Steam store Description is the only authoritative source of lore. Steam news updates, in-game codex entries, or developer posts that go beyond the description are not admitted as lore evidence as of 2026-09-30.\n\nOlivenia, Noah, and the escort mission\n\nThe store Description introduces Olivenia as the operative moving through the city, and Noah as her handler who gives remote guidance. The description frames Olivenia as the active mover (the player-side character), while Noah stays off-site and acts as the voice on the radio. The store does not publish a deeper dossier for either character at this time, so the dynamic should be read as an operator-handler pairing rather than as a full character biography.\n\nThe description positions the escort as the spine of the plot, with the city serving as the threat environment. If the Olivenia-and-Noah dynamic appears in a later Steam news update, a developer post, or an in-game codex, those would be the next authoritative sources for their backstory.\n\nBeatrice and the biological-weapon reveal\n\nThe store Description mentions a researcher named Beatrice as a reveal hook tied to the escort mission, and hints at a biological weapon somewhere in the city. The description does not detail Beatrice's affiliation, motivations, or field of research, and it does not name or describe the biological weapon beyond the brief hook.\n\nFor the launch window, both elements should be read as story beats set up by the description rather than as solved mysteries. A reader who expects the Steam store Description to spell out Beatrice's role or the weapon's nature will not find that on the store page.\n\nThe zombie city setting\n\nThe store Description sets the stage as a city overrun by a zombie virus outbreak. The city is described as the environment Olivenia moves through while Noah guides her from a remote location. The description does not publish a city map, district names, named landmarks, an outbreak timeline, or a survivor roster, so the city backdrop should be read as a thematic setting rather than as a fully named game world.\n\nThe store Description also does not state whether the city is the same city in which Beatrice works, where the biological weapon is stored, or where Olivenia is being escorted from and to. Those coordinates belong to the reveal-hook layer of the story, not to a published map.\n\nWhat the story is not\n\nThe Tunnel Escape Fates Entwined story as published on the store page is not a co-op story (the store row is single-player only), not a romance-led narrative (no relationship systems are published), and not a branching chapter selecter (no ending list is published). The store Description also does not publish a post-credits scene description or a sequel hook."
